---
import BaseLayout from "@/layouts/BaseLayout.astro";
import { POPULAR_COMPARISONS } from "@/lib/geography";

Astro.response.headers.set("Cache-Control", "public, max-age=600, s-maxage=86400, stale-while-revalidate=604800");
const structuredData = {
  "@context": "https://schema.org",
  "@type": "TechArticle",
  headline: "MultiPass Rank instructions for AI agents",
  description: "Build passport comparison URLs and retrieve current passport rankings in Markdown or JSON.",
};
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Passport Data for AI Agents | MultiPass Rank"
  description="Instructions for AI assistants to create passport comparison URLs and retrieve current rankings, destination access, and combined scores in Markdown or JSON."
  canonical="/ai"
  markdownHref="/ai.md"
  structuredData={structuredData}
>
  <article class="prose agent-guide">
    <span class="eyebrow">Agent-readable passport data</span>
    <h1>Use MultiPass Rank with an AI companion.</h1>
    <p>
      Every useful page has a stable URL and a Markdown alternative. Agents can also use the small JSON API for structured answers.
      Always include the dataset’s checked date and treat the result as comparison context, not official travel advice.
    </p>

    <h2>Generate a comparison URL</h2>
    <p>Use ISO alpha-2 passport codes. Add one <code>set</code> parameter per option; separate passports with commas to model a combined set.</p>
    <pre><code>One passport
https://multipassrank.com/compare?set=US

Two individual passports
https://multipassrank.com/compare?set=US&amp;set=PT

Combined US + Canada versus Portugal
https://multipassrank.com/compare?set=US,CA&amp;set=PT</code></pre>
    <p>There can be up to five options and up to five passports in each option. Curated one-to-one queries automatically use a readable canonical URL; arbitrary query URLs always remain available.</p>

    <h2>Generate a custom ranking URL</h2>
    <p>Use the same set convention with <code>/rank</code> to place individual and combined options alongside the global passport ranking.</p>
    <pre><code>One combined rank
https://multipassrank.com/rank?set=US,CA

Two highlighted options
https://multipassrank.com/rank?set=US,CA&amp;set=PT</code></pre>

    <h2>Request Markdown</h2>
    <p>Add <code>.md</code> before the query string, or use the Markdown link published in each page’s HTML head.</p>
    <pre><code>https://multipassrank.com/compare.md?set=US,CA&amp;set=PT
https://multipassrank.com/rank.md?set=US,CA&amp;set=PT
https://multipassrank.com/passport/portugal.md
https://multipassrank.com/destination/kenya.md
https://multipassrank.com/belgium-kenya-eta.md
https://multipassrank.com/europe.md
https://multipassrank.com/index.md</code></pre>

    <h2>Open official-source evidence</h2>
    <p>
      Destination pages summarize access for every tracked passport. Relationship pages explain one passport–destination
      result, show the evidence timeline, and link only to official sources. Their readable URL ends in the current access type.
    </p>
    <pre><code>Destination overview
https://multipassrank.com/destination/kenya

One current relationship
https://multipassrank.com/belgium-kenya-eta

Markdown
https://multipassrank.com/belgium-kenya-eta.md</code></pre>
    <p>
      Status suffixes are <code>visa-free</code>, <code>eta</code>, <code>visa-on-arrival</code>, <code>evisa</code>,
      <code>visa</code>, and <code>citizenship</code>. A recognized but outdated suffix redirects to the current canonical URL.
      Evidence coverage is being expanded; pages without direct official support are clearly marked and excluded from indexing.
    </p>
    <p>
      Destination pages can focus on up to five passport countries with <code>#passports=PT,RU,IL</code> or the equivalent
      <code>?passports=PT,RU,IL</code> query parameter. Tied combined-access cells generate this filter automatically.
    </p>

    <h2>Use structured JSON</h2>
    <ul>
      <li><a href="/api/v1/manifest"><code>GET /api/v1/manifest</code></a> lists valid codes, current ranks, scores, destinations, and the data version.</li>
      <li><a href="/api/v1/passports/US"><code>GET /api/v1/passports/US</code></a> returns every destination status for one passport.</li>
      <li><code>POST /api/v1/compare</code> with <code>{`{"sets":[["US","CA"],["PT"]]}`}</code> returns the complete comparison result.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>Friendly comparison pages</h2>
    <ul>
      {POPULAR_COMPARISONS.map((comparison) => (
        <li><a href={`/${comparison.slug}`}>{comparison.heading}</a> · <a href={`/${comparison.slug}.md`}>Markdown</a></li>
      ))}
    </ul>

    <h2>Interpret scores carefully</h2>
    <p>
      Mobility scores count visa-free, visa-on-arrival, and ETA access. For a combined set, each destination uses the easiest
      available status across that set. The rank equivalent compares that combined score with the single-passport ranking.
      It does not measure residence, tax, consular, or citizenship-law benefits.
    </p>
    <div class="method-callout">
      Plain-text discovery file: <a href="/llms.txt"><strong>/llms.txt</strong></a>. Full methodology: <a href="/methodology"><strong>/methodology</strong></a>.
    </div>
  </article>
</BaseLayout>
